The '''Cerroitá Pass''' ({{Literal translation|big mountain}}) is a [[Matarrañya|Matarrañyan]] pass which has been used since colonial times as the most direct link between the [[Aipi]] basin on the Bayaran side of the Matarrañya / Cañadapirén and the [[W:Atlantic|Atlantic]] side of [[Atlántida]], avoiding the x-day, {{convert|510.80|km|mi|0|abbr=on}} detour through the less challenging [[Guadazequias valley]]. In 1828 it was used by the [[Army of the Matarrañya]] to cross the Matarrañya, in their [[Siege of Castejón|campaign to seize]] the [[Alpujarra]] and split the [[Southern Confederation]].

Since the 1880s, it was the most used pass in all of the [[Baliscan archipelago]]. Today, a railroad tunnel built by the [[Baliscan Railways Group|Transmatarrañyan Railway]] (1910–present) runs underneath, while the [[Autopista 208]] runs through the nearby [[La Joya Tunnel]]''.'' 
